# LSAN (LeakSanitizer) suppression file
# suppresses known memory leaks from third-party libraries

# tcmalloc library initialization allocates internal data structures
# that are not freed on program exit, causing LSAN to report leaks.
# This is expected behavior and not a real memory leak.
# See: https://github.com/gperftools/gperftools
leak:MallocExtension::Initialize

# The string returned by GetHeapProfile() is intentionally not freed.
#
# Its header says the caller should free() it, but there is no portable
# deallocator: with a statically linked tcmalloc the buffer comes from the
# library's own allocator, which the process's malloc interposer never saw, so
# free() aborts with "attempting free on address which was not malloc()-ed".
# gperftools 2.18 additionally routes the intermediate chunks through an internal
# arena, tc_free() only exists from 2.16.90, and LowLevelAlloc::Free lives in a
# private header. The buffer is ~11KB per stop, so retaining it is the safe trade.
leak:GetHeapProfile
